    ('389L','CRJU','15017','Why do individuals participate in politics and public life This is an important question, since much of public policy depends both on individual support and relies on the actions of a variety of public and private organizations. This course will explore the motivations and constraints behind various kinds of civic engagement. There will be a focus on the interaction between a variety of public and private organizations examining various influences in and by public decisionmaking.','ST:CRJU'),
    ('390L','CRJU','11776','ST Hudson Valley Natural HistoryThis course is for those who want to learn about the natural world while being in the natural world. We spend about  of class time outdoors examining whats living there and the features of the surrounding habitats. Our time is a blend of enjoying where we are, exploring interesting and scenic locations, plus gaining scientific knowledge of living and nonliving features of the different ecosystems. We focus on taxonomy  identifying species  with a special focus on the diversity of beautiful early autumn wildflowers and the ever present tree species. Tree identification and dendrology more broadly gets additional attention, as we learn their identities even after leaffall by twigs, bark and seeds. Fish, birds and insects also get attention, as well as field geology and fresh waters. You will learn the methods of field identification with the use of field guides, taxonomic keys, magnifiers and binoculars. Identifications are confirmed as needed in the lab. You will also learn the methods of preservation, labeling and presentation of flowering plant specimens, as you create your own collections. We explore many different sites in the Hudson Valley, north and south of campus and on both sides of the river.','ST:CRIMINAL JUSTICE'),
