    ('391N','MDIA','5246','STHUMAN RIGHTS IN LATIN AMERICAIn the late twentieth century, hundreds of thousands of people across Latin America were murdered, tortured or disappeared by authoritarian governments. In response, the people of the region built a movement for justice, human rights and democracy that made human rights one of the core ways we think about global justice and progress. In this class, we will examine Latin Americans unique role over the course of five centuries  from the colonial era to the twentieth century  in the formation of human rights ideas and in the creation of political practices and legal institutions aimed at securing those rights. We will explore the difficult histories of Cold War violence across Latin America, and examine how the problems of truth, justice and reconciliation loomed over the region with the return to democracy in the s and s. We will conclude by examining the complex question of what justice means in sharply unequal societies still burdened by painful legacies of state violence and repression.','INDEPENDENT STUDY:MDIA'),
